# plugin/commands/ — slash commands

Every `.md` file here becomes a slash command namespaced by the plugin name, so
`status.md` is invoked as **`/laika:status`**.

That is why this file has **no `.md` extension**. Named `README.md` it registers
as a junk `/laika:README` command — verified, not assumed: invoking it expanded
`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` and ran through the command pipeline. Unlike `skills/`,
where a loose `.md` is inert, `commands/` scans every `.md` it finds. Do not add
one here that is not meant to be a command.

## Here now

| Command | File | Script | Landed in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `/laika:status` | `status.md` | `laika-status.sh` | LAI-012, capacity in LAI-420 |
| `/laika:setup` | `setup.md` | `laika-setup.sh` | LAI-420 |
| `/laika:tasks` | `tasks.md` | — (MCP tool) | LAI-420 |
| `/laika:standup` | `standup.md` | `laika-standup.sh` | LAI-420 |

All four of SPEC §8's commands are here.

**`/laika:tasks` runs no script.** It calls the `list_ready_tasks` MCP tool,
which already returns markdown sorted by §4.5's rule. A script would re-sort what
the server sorted — a second definition of "ready" that can disagree with the
board's own column.

**`/laika:setup` configures nothing** (D-046). `npx laika init` owns
configuration; this reports what is set and prints the command. A second minting
path would make LAI-422's idempotence criterion unprovable rather than merely
unmet — "already configured" would mean "configured somewhere I happen to look".

**`/laika:standup` says what it is showing.** There is no per-actor activity
endpoint, so it reads the org feed for 24 hours and selects the caller's rows;
when the window returns a full page it says older rows were not read, rather than
implying the list is complete.

Every script shares `scripts/laika-common.sh`, which holds the two messages that
would otherwise be written three times: **which variable is missing**, and
**your token was refused** as distinct from **the board is unreachable**
(LAI-224's lesson, in the plugin). Each exits 0 — a command that explains itself
beats one that hands the agent an error to interpret.
